{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically favoring the one that is more left-skewed (i.e., where favorable outcomes are relatively more probable compared to unfavorable ones). To assess skewness in this context, we need to evaluate the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Both lotteries have only two outcomes: a positive monetary outcome and zero. Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 0.2% probability; Lottery B offers 6000 Euros with 0.1% probability. Both have near-certain zero outcomes (99.8% and 99.9%, respectively). Since the only non-zero outcomes are gains (favorable), and both lotteries assign very small probabilities to these gains, the concept of skewness is limited in this binary-outcome context. However, Lottery A assigns twice the probability (0.2% vs. 0.1%) to its gain, though at half the amount. In terms of probability mass on favorable outcomes, Lottery A has a higher probability of a gain, making it relatively more left-skewed than Lottery B. Thus, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A.",
