  "step_2": "The verbal report is: 'Summe bei B ist das 0,1 Prozent weniger Risko weniger nicht wert' (translated: 'For B, the 0.1 percent less risk is not worth it'). The individual appears to be comparing the risk levels of the two lotteries, noting that Lottery B has 0.1% less probability of a negative outcome (though the exact magnitude of the loss is not mentioned). The individual considers the reduction in risk (0.1% lower chance of a loss) but concludes it is 'not worth it'—implying a trade-off against something else (possibly the magnitude of the loss, though not explicitly stated). The justification focuses on the difference in risk (probability of loss) and evaluates whether a small reduction in risk justifies the choice. The individual seems to be weighing probability of loss against some other factor, possibly the severity of the loss, though this is not clearly articulated. The deliberation centers on risk magnitude in terms of probability, not explicitly on distribution shape or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness—specifically, which lottery concentrates more probability on favorable outcomes. In this case, Lottery B is more left-skewed because it has a lower probability of a loss (0.1% vs 0.2%), even though the loss is larger. The individual in the verbal report notices this lower risk (0.1% less probability of a negative outcome) but dismisses it as 'not worth it'. This suggests the individual is aware of the lower probability of loss in B but chooses A (implied by rejecting B), possibly due to the larger loss amount in B. The information about the probability distribution (and thus skewness) is partially considered (the lower probability of loss in B), but the justification is not based on preferring more left-skewed distributions; rather, it seems to trade off probability against outcome severity. The decision reason would prefer B, but the individual appears to reject B despite recognizing its lower risk. Therefore, while the individual considers a component of skewness (probability of loss), they do not use skewness as a decision rule—they seem to discount the lower risk due to the larger loss. Hence, the decision reason is not used in the deliberation.",
